---
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "Remote Work Taxes: The No-BS Guide (2025)";
const description =
  "Taxes as a nomad are confusing as hell. Tax home, residency, foreign income - here's what actually matters and what you can ignore without IRS trouble.";
const author = "FloatJet Team";
const authorUrl = "/about";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-11-23");
const readingTime = 4;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Calculator and financial documents for tax planning";

// Table of contents headings
const headings = [
  { text: "Why This Is Complicated", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Tax Residency", slug: "tax-residency", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Your Situation", slug: "tax-scenarios", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Deductions Worth Knowing", slug: "deductions", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The International Stuff", slug: "international", depth: 2 },
  { text: "When to Get Help", slug: "hire-professional", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Answers", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Bottom Line", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Do I pay taxes if I work from another country?",
    answer: "<p>Usually yes. Depends on tax residency, not location. US citizens always file.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Home office deduction?",
    answer: "<p>Self-employed: yes, if exclusive business use. Employees: no (federal).</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Coworking deductible?",
    answer: "<p>Self-employed: yes. Keep receipts.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Foreign bank accounts?",
    answer: "<p>Report them. Over $10,000 = FBAR required. Penalties are brutal.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Nomad travel deductible?",
    answer: "<p>No. Business travel yes. Lifestyle travel no.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Quarterly taxes?",
    answer: "<p>Freelancers: pay quarterly or face penalties. Set aside 25-30% of everything.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Banking Solutions for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt:
      "Compare international-friendly banks, multi-currency accounts, and fee-free options for remote workers.",
    href: "/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1563013544-824ae1b704d3-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Digital banking on smartphone",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Productivity Apps for Remote Workers",
    excerpt:
      "Stay organized and efficient with these top-rated productivity tools designed for distributed teams.",
    href: "/tools/best-productivity-apps",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1484480974693-6ca0a78fb36b-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Productivity apps on laptop screen",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Time Tracking Software",
    excerpt:
      "Track billable hours accurately and understand where your time goes with these top time tracking tools.",
    href: "/tools/best-time-tracking-software",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1508672019048-805c876b67e2-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Time tracking dashboard",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Guides"
    categorySlug="guides"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Why This Is Complicated</h2>
  <p>
    Beach in Lisbon. Great coffee. Tax season hits. Suddenly you're googling "do I pay taxes in Portugal?" at 2am.
  </p>
  <p>
    Remote work doesn't simplify taxes. It makes them way more complicated.
  </p>
  <p>
    I spent three years figuring this out the hard way. Accountant bills. Late penalties. One very stressful call with
    the IRS.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's what I wish someone had told me.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> Not tax advice. Laws change. Get a professional for your specific situation.
  </p>

  <!-- Understanding Tax Residency -->
  <h2 id="tax-residency">Tax Residency (The Thing That Actually Matters)</h2>
  <p>
    This is the whole game. Where are you tax resident?
  </p>
  <p>
    It's not where your passport says. It's not where you're sitting right now. It's where the tax authorities say you
    belong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most countries use the 183-day rule. Spend more than half the year somewhere? Probably tax resident there.
  </p>
  <p>
    But there's also: where's your home base, where's your family, where are your bank accounts, where do you intend to
    return?
  </p>

  <h3>US Citizens: You're Special (Not in a Good Way)</h3>
  <p>
    America and Eritrea. That's it. Only two countries that tax citizens no matter where they live.
  </p>
  <p>
    Living in Bali for five years? Still filing US taxes. Haven't been home since 2019? Still filing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The good news: <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/foreign-earned-income-exclusion"
                      target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Foreign Earned Income Exclusion lets you exclude up to $126,500
    (2024)</a> according to the IRS. Foreign Tax Credit helps
    avoid double taxation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bad news: Miss a form and you lose these benefits. The paperwork is brutal.
  </p>

  <h3>Breaking Ties</h3>
  <p>
    Want to stop being tax resident somewhere? It's not as simple as leaving.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>UK:</strong> Fewer than 16-90 days per year (depends on your ties)</li>
    <li><strong>Canada:</strong> Sever residential ties—no home, no spouse there</li>
    <li><strong>Germany:</strong> Deregister your address, don't keep a place</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Get this wrong and you're paying taxes to a country you left years ago.
  </p>

  <!-- Common Tax Scenarios -->
  <h2 id="tax-scenarios">Your Situation</h2>

  <h3>W-2 Employee Working Remote</h3>
  <p>
    Straightforward. Employer withholds everything. File normally.
  </p>
  <p>
    Watch out: Move states mid-year? You might owe both states. Tell your employer.
  </p>

  <h3>Freelancer</h3>
  <p>
    More complicated. You handle:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Self-employment tax (15.3% on top of income tax)</li>
    <li>Quarterly payments (April, June, September, January)</li>
    <li>Tracking every expense</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Track everything. <a href="/tools/best-accounting-software-freelancers">QuickBooks, FreshBooks</a>, whatever. Just
    do it all year, not in April.
  </p>

  <h3>Nomad Bouncing Between Countries</h3>
  <p>
    Here's where it gets messy.
  </p>
  <p>
    You might be tax resident nowhere. Or everywhere. Or in some gray area that keeps you up at night.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tax authorities share information now. Playing jurisdictional games is risky. Pick a tax home.
  </p>

  <!-- Remote Work Deductions -->
  <h2 id="deductions">Deductions Worth Knowing</h2>
  <p>
    Self-employed only. W-2 employees lost most deductions in 2018.
  </p>

  <h3>Home Office</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Simple method: <a
        href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/simplified-option-for-home-office-deduction"
        target="_blank" rel="nofollow">$5/sq ft, up to 300 sq ft ($1,500 max)</a> per the IRS
    </li>
    <li>Regular method: Actual expenses, proportional to office size</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Regular method is more work but usually more money back. See our complete <a
      href="/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide">freelancer tax deductions guide</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h3>Equipment</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/guides/can-you-write-off-home-office-equipment">Laptop, monitor, chair, desk</a>—all deductible. <a
      href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p946" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Section
    179 lets you deduct the full cost immediately</a> according to IRS rules.
  </p>

  <h3>Software</h3>
  <p>
    Notion, Slack, Zoom, <a href="/tools/best-project-management-software">project management tools</a>. All business
    expenses.
  </p>

  <h3>Coworking</h3>
  <p>
    Memberships, day passes—deductible. That $5 coffee while working? Technically deductible (but keep receipts).
  </p>

  <h3>What's NOT Deductible</h3>
  <p>
    Flying to Bali because you want to. Living in Lisbon because it's nice. Your nomad lifestyle is personal, not
    business.
  </p>
  <p>
    Client meeting in another city? Deductible. Working poolside because Instagram? Not deductible.
  </p>

  <!-- International Considerations -->
  <h2 id="international">The International Stuff</h2>

  <h3>FBAR (Don't Skip This)</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/report-of-foreign-bank-and-financial-accounts-fbar"
       target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Foreign accounts over $10,000 total? File an FBAR</a> according to IRS
    requirements. Every year.
  </p>
  <p>
    Penalties for not filing: up to $12,909 per account. Willful violations? 50% of your account balance.
  </p>
  <p>
    I know people who got hit with this. It's not fun.
  </p>
  <p>
    For help managing international finances, see our guide to
    <a href="/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads">banking for digital nomads</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Tax Treaties</h3>
  <p>
    The US has treaties with 60+ countries to prevent double taxation. Check if yours applies.
  </p>

  <h3>VAT</h3>
  <p>
    Selling to consumers in the EU? You might need to collect VAT. B2B is usually exempt. This is complex—get help.
  </p>

  <!-- When to Hire a Professional -->
  <h2 id="hire-professional">When to Get Help</h2>
  <p>
    DIY works if:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You stayed in one country</li>
    <li>Single income source</li>
    <li>No foreign accounts</li>
    <li>Straightforward situation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Get a professional if:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Multiple countries</li>
    <li>Foreign bank accounts</li>
    <li>US citizen abroad</li>
    <li>Multiple income sources</li>
    <li>Setting up an LLC or S-Corp</li>
    <li>Behind on filings</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What It Costs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Simple return: $300-500</li>
    <li>International complexity: $500-1,500</li>
    <li>Full expat prep: $800-2,500+</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Worth it. A good <a href="/tools/best-accounting-software-freelancers">accountant finds deductions</a> you missed
    and keeps you out of trouble.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    Tax residency matters most. Know where you belong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Track everything all year. Not in April.
  </p>
  <p>
    Foreign accounts? Report them. Seriously.
  </p>
  <p>
    Get professional help if it's complicated. The cost is worth avoiding penalties.
  </p>
  <p>
    The worst move is ignoring it and hoping nobody notices. They notice now. International info sharing is real.
  </p>
  <p>
    Figure it out. Your future self will thank you.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
